    labels:
      - Lab
      - Bus stop
    values:
      - 28
      - 0
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: '%'
      decimals: 0
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 40
    y_label: Share given away
    gap: 0.45
    box:
      x: 110
      'y': 160
      w: 530
      h: 420
sources:
    - id: engel2011
      title: 'Dictator games: a meta study'
      authors: Christoph Engel
      year: 2011
      finding: Experimental Economics 14:583–610. Over more than a hundred published dictator-game experiments, dictators give on average about 28% of the pie; about 64% give something.
    - id: winking2013
      title: Natural-field dictator game shows no altruistic giving
      authors: Jeffrey Winking, Nicholas Mizer
      year: 2013
      finding: Evolution and Human Behavior 34(4):288–293. At a Las Vegas bus stop a confederate late for the airport handed strangers $20 in casino chips beside a second stranger; in the two natural-field conditions no one shared any chips; when told they were in a study and could share, most shared some.

    text q0 "Give money\nto a stranger?" #2f6b8f x 830 y 500 font_size 34 hidden true
    text rules "Give any share\nto a stranger\nNo veto" #8f887c cites ["engel2011"] x 830 y 500 font_size 30 hidden true
    icon busstop size 90 set twemoji x 770 y 560 or ["bus"] icon_look picture of "bus stop" hidden true
    icon p1 size 80 set twemoji x 850 y 560 or ["person"] icon_look picture of "person standing" hidden true
    icon p2 size 80 set twemoji x 920 y 560 or ["person"] icon_look picture of "person standing" hidden true
    text vegas "$20 in casino chips\nLas Vegas" #8f887c cites ["winking2013"] x 830 y 440 font_size 28 hidden true
    text none "not one shared" #b5482e cites ["winking2013"] x 830 y 330 font_size 32 hidden true
    text study "told it's a study\n→ most gave" #2f6b8f cites ["winking2013"] x 830 y 470 font_size 30 hidden true
    text stamp "28 or 0?" #b5482e x 830 y 420 font_size 64 hidden true
    text policy "letters · defaults · taxes" #3d3833 x 830 y 250 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Same game, two answers"

How do we test a theory of how people choose, and when should we believe the result? Take one simple question:  (@draw q0@)will people give money to a stranger?
    draw axes

B: Take the dictator game, t (@erase q0@)he standard l (@draw rules@)ab test of giving: you may hand any share of some money to a stranger (@highlight rules effect underline@), who can't refuse.

Across more than a hundre (@draw bar_1@)d lab studies, dictators give about twenty-eight percent. Hold that number.

    erase rules

B: Now play it where nobody knows it's a game. In L (@draw busstop@)as Vegas, Jeffrey Winking and Nicholas Mizer had a man in a hurry for the airport hand a stranger at a bu (@draw vegas@)s stop twenty dollars in casino chips.

Anothe (@draw p1 p2 parallel true@)r stranger stood right beside (@highlight p2 effect circle@) them. Sharing was easy.

B: Less than the lab, surely. But I'd still bet on a few dollars.
    highlight bar_1

    ask "Drag the bus-stop bar to your guess: the share given away." on bar_2 store g default "15"
        right: Zero: nobody shared anything.
        wrong: You said {g}: it was zero.

    erase vegas busstop p1 p2

Not a cent. Not one stran (@draw none@)ger shared a single chip.

    erase none

B: Then the researchers ran it again, telling people it was a s (@draw study@)tudy and that they could share. Now most of them gave some.

Same city, same chips. What changed was knowing it was a study, and being told you could share.
    highlight study

    erase study

B: So which number is the truth about generosity, twenty-eigh (@draw stamp@)t or zero?

It matters well beyond one game. Governments no (@draw policy@)w design letters, defaults and taxes on evidence like this, and the wrong number sends (@highlight stamp@) a policy the wrong way.

So the question is not just what people do. It is what each way of watching them can show.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2

## Control or realism
sources:
    - id: harrison2004
      title: Field Experiments
      authors: Glenn W. Harrison, John A. List
      year: 2004
      finding: Journal of Economic Literature 42(4):1009–1055. Classifies experiments from the conventional lab through artefactual field experiments (lab task, non-student subjects), framed field experiments (field commodity, task or context; subjects know they are studied) to natural field experiments (natural setting, subjects do not know).

    box b1 "Lab" #b5482e x 100 y 480 width 180 height 150 radius 10 font_size 32 in rowg hidden true
    box b2 "Lab game,\nreal people" #3d3833 cites ["harrison2004"] x 295 y 480 width 180 height 150 radius 10 font_size 32 in rowg hidden true
