    card title "Control or realism"

The lab and the bus st (@draw b1@)op sit at two ends of one li (@draw b4 tag@)ne, and every test of a theory of choice sits somewhere on it.

B: At one end, the lab: volunteers, usually students, at a screen, playing by rules the researcher writes.
    highlight b1

Its strength is c (@draw ctl ctl_l@)ontrol. Change one rule, hold everything else fixed, and any difference is that rule's doing.

B: In 2004 Glenn  (@highlight b1@)Harrison and John List mapped the steps out of the lab. First, the same  (@draw b2@)game, played by traders or villagers instead of students.

Next, real goods (@draw b3@) and a real task, but people still know they are being studied.

B: Last, the natural  (@draw brk fe@)field experiment: a real setting, a change (@highlight b4@) assigned at random, and nobody knows. The bus s (@highlight tag effect circle@)top was one.

Beyond the experiments lies natural  (@draw b5@)data. Nobody assigns anything; we watch what people do, and hunt for accidents  (@highlight b5 effect underline@)that work like an experiment.

B: Two questio (@highlight ctl@)ns decide what each can show. Internal validity (@draw iv@): did the change really cause the result, here?

And extern (@draw real@)al validity: would the resul (@draw ev@)t hold somewhere else, with other people and real stakes?

Moving right b (@highlight real ctl@)uys realism and pays for it in control. Randomise, and you keep i (@highlight b4 effect underline@)nternal validity; give up randomising, and you must argue for it.

    hide b1 b2 b3 b4 b5 brk fe tag ctl ctl_l iv real ev

    draw sortq

    ask "Sort each study: lab, field experiment, or natural data." on sortq store srt stage own voice b
        intro: Your turn. Here are famous studies of choice. Sort each one: lab, field experiment, or natural data.
        right: Lab: markets and the ultimatum game. Field: the day-care fine, the card swap, the nudge letters. Natural data: the pension switch, the diplomats, the election-year police.
        wrong: {srt} of {srt.total} on the first try. Nobody assigned the pension switch, the diplomats' tickets or the police. Here: lab, markets and ultimatum; field, day-care fine, card swap and letters; natural data, the other three.

    show b1 b2 b3 b4 b5 brk fe tag ctl ctl_l iv real ev

Nobody assigne (@highlight b5@)d the pension default or the diplomats' tickets. Each needed a careful case that nothing else changed at the same moment.

B: So no method wins on every count. Even th (@highlight b1 b5@)e natural field experiment pays: one setting, few levers to pull, and it rarely tells you why.

    hide tag

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own
        intro: Why does randomising buy internal validity?

@math

    erase mathq

    hide real ev ctl ctl_l iv

Compare those who got a change with those who didn't.
    draw mw_box mw_line_1

The gap is the effect, plus however the two groups differed anyway.
    draw mw_line_2

B: Pick the groups by coin flip, and that second term is zero on average.
    draw mw_line_3

Without a coin, as in natural data, you have to argue it away.
    draw mw_line_4

    erase mw

@test

    erase mathq

    show real ev ctl ctl_l iv tag

## Does the lab lie?
sources:
    - id: levitt2007
      title: What Do Laboratory Experiments Measuring Social Preferences Reveal About the Real World?
      authors: Steven D. Levitt, John A. List
      year: 2007
      finding: Journal of Economic Perspectives 21(2):153–174. Lab choices depend on the stakes, the nature and extent of scrutiny, the context, and how participants and tasks are selected; where lab and field differ on these, results need not carry over.
    - id: list2006
      title: 'The Behavioralist Meets the Market: Measuring Social Preferences and Reputation Effects in Actual Transactions'
      authors: John A. List
      year: 2006
      finding: Journal of Political Economy 114(1):1–37. Sportscard dealers reciprocate in lab-style treatments but behave closer to self-interest in natural settings; higher quality where reputation (local dealers) or third-party verification is at stake.
    - id: camerer2015
      title: 'The Promise and Success of Lab-Field Generalizability in Experimental Economics: A Critical Reply to Levitt and List'
      authors: Colin F. Camerer
      year: 2015
      finding: In Fréchette & Schotter (eds), Handbook of Experimental Economic Methodology, OUP (working paper 2011). Generalizability is not the primary goal of lab experiments (a 'scientific' rather than 'policy' view); most lab features that may limit it are not inherent; there is no replicated evidence that lab data fail to generalize when lab and field are closely matched.
    - id: franzen2013
      title: The external validity of giving in the dictator game
      authors: Axel Franzen, Sonja Pointner
      year: 2013
      finding: Experimental Economics 16(2):155–169. Subjects who gave in a double-blind lab dictator game returned a misdirected letter containing money (sent 4–5 weeks or two years later) more often than those who gave nothing.
    - id: alubaydli2013
      title: 'On the Generalizability of Experimental Results in Economics: With a Response to Camerer'
      authors: Omar Al-Ubaydli, John A. List
      year: 2013
      finding: 'NBER Working Paper 19666. Reply to Camerer: scrutiny and self-selection make lab point estimates of social preferences unreliable guides to the field; both sides agree more lab-field bridges are needed.'

    path div #8f887c points [[500,640],[500,330]] hidden true
    text hl "Levitt & List" #b5482e cites ["levitt2007"] x 250 y 620 font_size 36 hidden true
    text c1 "watched" #3d3833 cites ["levitt2007"] x 250 y 555 font_size 32 hidden true
    text c2 "small stakes" #3d3833 x 250 y 505 font_size 32 hidden true
    text c3 "odd setting" #3d3833 x 250 y 455 font_size 32 hidden true
