    text bridge "gave in lab → returned letter more often" #3d3833 cites ["franzen2013"] x 560 y 275 font_size 30 hidden true
    text lvl "ranking travelled ✓" #b5482e cites ["franzen2013"] x 500 y 215 font_size 38 hidden true
    text field "but field: one city, one year" #8f887c x 750 y 370 font_size 28 hidden true
    text s1 "trade-off: mostly agreed" #3d3833 x 500 y 300 font_size 36 hidden true
    text s2 "lab numbers in the world: contested" #b5482e cites ["alubaydli2013"] x 500 y 250 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Does the lab lie?"

B: Twenty-eight in the lab, zero at the bus stop. In 2007 Steven Levitt  (@draw div hl@)and John List argued that gaps like this are no accident.

In the lab  (@draw c1@)someone is watching, the s (@draw c2@)takes are small, the settin (@draw c3@)g is strange, and the peopl (@draw c4@)e chose to come.

B: List had seen it on the same card-show floors he'd long studie (@draw cards@)d. Dealers who repaid a generous buyer in a lab version gave lower quality on the show floor, unless th (@highlight c1 effect underline@)ey were locals with a reputation to keep.

    erase cards

Colin C (@draw hr@)amerer replied that the lab never promised to measure how generous the world is. Its job is to te (@draw r1@)st principles: what pushes giving up or down.

B: And he found no replicated case of the lab failing when (@draw r2@) lab and field were closely matched.

Axel Franzen and Sonja Pointner tested it on the same people.  (@draw labdg@)They played a dictator game in the lab; weeks later, a misdirected let (@draw letter@)ter with money inside reached their mailbox.

B: Those who had given something in t (@draw bridge@)he lab returned the letter more often than those who gave nothing. Levels may not travel, as the bus stop sho (@draw lvl ends@)wed, but the ranking did.

And the field has a blind spot of its own: the bus stop i (@draw field@)s one city, one year, one kind of stranger. That is an external- (@highlight field@)validity problem too.

    erase letter bridge lvl labdg

B: How sure are we? That each method trades control for realism, and the best answers combine the (@draw s1 ends@)m: mostly agreed.

How far the lab's numbers c (@draw s2@)arry into the world: contested, and argued one study at a time.

    quiz "A library texts a randomly chosen half of its overdue borrowers a reminder. Nobody is told it's a study. What kind of test is it?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: A natural field experiment: a change assigned at random, in real life, to people who don't know.
        wrong: The library assigned the texts at random, in a real setting, to people who didn't know. Here, a natural field experiment.
        * Lab experiment
        + Natural field experiment
        * Natural data

    quiz "Students in a lab give 30% to a charity, knowing the researcher sees it. A charity plans its mailing on that number. The biggest worry?" voice b
        right: External validity: the cause is clear in the lab, but being watched may not travel to a letter opened at home.
        wrong: Inside the lab the cause is clear; the doubt is whether it travels to people at home. Here, external validity.
        * Internal validity
        + External validity
        * Too few students

    quiz "Towns that chose a sugar tax drink less soda than towns that didn't. The main worry?"
        right: Internal validity: the towns chose the tax, and health-minded towns may drink less soda anyway.
        wrong: The towns picked themselves, and may differ anyway. Here, internal validity.
        + Internal validity
        * External validity
        * None, it's real data

    erase s1 s2 div hl c1 c2 c3 c4 hr r1 r2 field

So how do we test a theory of choice? With all three: th (@draw k1@)e lab to find the cause, the field to se (@draw k2@)e if it survives, natural data to see ho (@draw k3@)w big it is in the wild.

B: And when they disagree, as at the bus stop, the gap is (@highlight k1 k2 k3@) a finding in itself. It shows what the lab left out.

